course: dictionary
lesson: 10043
title: Terrible: more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Terrible» is one of those words. It usually means very bad or unpleasant, but it can also mean very serious and frightening, or very great, as in «a terrible hurry».
explain: Read the words around it to choose. «They bake terrible cookies, and it's the best afternoon they've had.» Here terrible means bad: the cookies were not good, but the afternoon was still fun. «He was terrible in goal» means he was very bad at doing something.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. Put "very bad" into «a terrible storm» and it still makes sense. Put "very great" into «a terrible hurry» and it fits too. When a word has several meanings, the sentence picks the right one.
fact: Terrible means very bad or unpleasant, as in «a terrible smell».
fact: Terrible can mean very serious and causing great harm or fear, as in «a terrible accident».
fact: «Terrible at» something means very bad at doing it, as in «terrible at maths».
fact: Informally, terrible can mean very great, as in «a terrible hurry» or «terribly sorry».
fact: Writers can use stronger or more exact words instead, such as dreadful, awful or horrifying.
exercise: «The future of AI is probably neither all wonderful nor all terrible; it depends on the choices people make.» What does terrible mean here? | Very funny | Very good at something | Very great, like a terrible hurry | *Very bad or unpleasant
exercise: Mr Pryce wrote that the officer «was terrible in goal». What does terrible mean here? | Very tasty | *Very bad at doing something | Very serious and causing great harm | Very quick and clever
exercise: Ella says, «I'm in a terrible hurry!» What does terrible mean? | *Very great | Very old | Very bad or unpleasant | Very wet
test: «The lorry was in a terrible accident on the motorway.» What does terrible mean in this sentence? | Very tidy | Very quiet | Very tasty | *Very serious and causing great harm
test: Sam says, «I feel terrible today.» What does terrible mean? | Very kind to others | *Feeling very bad or unwell | Very great, like a terrible hurry | Very happy and full of energy
exam: «The giant had terrible teeth as long as swords.» What does terrible mean here? | Very clean and shiny | Very kind and gentle | Very small and neat | *Very frightening and dangerous
